   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Lesson 3: Energy and Electrochemistry\n",
    "\n",
    "Electrochemistry is growing in popularity in academic, industrial and political circles. This is partially rooted in the utility of electrochemistry as an analytical technique, but the modern energy dilemma is arguably a larger driving force.\n",
    "\n",
    "Today's topics:\n",
    "- Overview of electrochemical energy storage\n",
    "- Guidance for topic selection (final project)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Reference:**\n",
    "- Chapter 1 in *Fundamental Concepts in Heterogeneous Catalysis*, Norskov, Studt, Abild-Pedersen & Bligaard.\n",
    "- The review articles listed at the end of this notebook.\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 1. Energy\n",
    "\n",
    "The first law of thermodynamics tells us that energy can be neither created nor destroyed. It can, however, be converted between numerous forms - a feature that we have exploited to harness energy for societal advancement. Increased quality of life has been intimately paired with increasing demand for energy. A steadily increasing quality of life for much of the planet has led to steadily increasing demand for energy. This increasing demand for energy has placed us in our current global situation: global demands to decrease reliance on fossil fuels without negatively impacting quality of life. \n",
    "\n",
    "There is an abundance of statistics that are available, sourced from private industry, government organizations and not-for-profit organizations. A sampling of these resources shows the trajectory and current state of the problem."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### BP Statistical Review of World Energy\n",
    "https://www.bp.com/en/global/corporate/energy-economics/statistical-review-of-world-energy.html\n",
    "\n",
    "The *BP Statistical Review of World Energy* provides an annual snapshot of global energy markets. Broken down by major energy source, the situation as of 2021 looks like this:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/primary-energy-world-consumption-stsr21-bp.svg\" width=40%>\n",
    "\n",
    "Notice that there are two dips: one caused by a global financial recession in 2008, and one by global economic shutdowns to slow down the advance of COVID-19 in 2020. Notice also the size of these dips and compare them to your own personal experiences over the past year. The world's population is increasing and energy supply for that population has been facilitated to the point that we hardly consider our own demands. We don't stop to think, for example, that every 👍 we post uses energy, or that storage of every digital image we take on the cloud results in continual energy consumption for the forseeable future!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The good news is that energy need not be a bad thing. Overreliance on fossil fuels is unsustainable and hazardous to our ecosystems, but it should be possible to transition to some form of sustainable energy environment. Inspection of the relative proportion of primary energy supplied by various sources does show that change is happening:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/shares-of-global-primary-energy-stsr21-bp.svg\" width=350>\n",
    "\n",
    "Fossil fuels still provide the overwhelming majority of global energy, and the share of renewables is only 5.7%...but that 5.7% represents a massive advance in the past 10 years. Particularly when one considers that oil and gas is both cheap to produce and a very well established global industry. Renewable energy sources have become a major player in global energy markets and are poised to take over!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The global perspective obviously lacks considerations of regional distributions:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/primary-energy-regional-consumption-pattern-2020-stsr21-bp.svg\" width=650>\n",
    "\n",
    "This chain of figures highlights the complexity of the problem. Different global regions have different needs, desires, and expectations. They also have different resources, economies and capabilities. It is naive to think that the problem can be solved directly. Unique solutions must be developed for unique regions. We should be optimistic about the future because the shape of our energy economy has become a topic of daily conversation and this attention leads to action. Consider:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Tesla sold its first Model S in 2012, when charging stations were unavailable across much of North America. There is now a network of >30,000 charging stations across North America.\n",
    "2. The European Union is assertively moving towards using hydrogen as a fuel, targeting 40,000 km of highways lined with fueling stations and repurposing pipelines for distribution. [Link](https://ec.europa.eu/info/law/better-regulation/have-your-say/initiatives/12407-Clean-energy-an-EU-hydrogen-strategy_en)\n",
    "3. Japan has a strategic target of 800,000 hydrogen fuel cell vehicles and 900 fueling stations in the country by 2030, along with 1,200 fuel cell buses. [Link](https://www.env.go.jp/seisaku/list/ondanka_saisei/lowcarbon-h2-sc/PDF/Summary_of_Japan's_Hydrogen_Strategy.pdf)\n",
    "\n",
    "**As scientists, our role is to explore, discover and optimize technologies that can drive this process.** We need to educate ourselves on strengths and limitations of relevant technologies, regional nuances, and take care to communicate in a clean, clear, and honest fashion.\n",
    "\n",
    "More detailed breakdowns of energy sources, regional variations, and modeling surrounding various technologies are also broadly available. Some key sources of information include:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### The [International Renewable Energy Agency](https://www.irena.org/statistics). \n",
    "\n",
    "Ongoing analysis of the global energy economy with a focus on renewables.\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/irena.png\" width=30%>\n",
    "\n",
    "The bars represent hydroelectric (blue), wind (gray), solar (yellow), bioenergy (green), and marine (dark red). [Interactive Source](https://public.tableau.com/app/profile/irena.resource/viz/IRENARETimeSeries/ExploreDashboard)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### [National Renewable Energy Labs](https://www.nrel.gov/). \n",
    "\n",
    "Detailed analysis of individual technolgies. For example, the figure below tracks the cost of electricity generated from commercial photovoltaics. [Source](https://www.nrel.gov/news/program/2021/documenting-a-decade-of-cost-declines-for-pv-systems.html)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/nrel-lcoe-pv.png\" width=50%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Such analyses make it obvious why renewables are growing: they are cost competitive with fossil fuels! \n",
    "\n",
    "For reference, a sample of the cost of electricity in various global jurisdictions is available [here](https://www.statista.com/statistics/263492/electricity-prices-in-selected-countries/):\n",
    "\n",
    "|Region| Cost (USD / kWh)|\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "|Germany | 0.37 |\n",
    "|Japan | 0.26 |\n",
    "|Australia | 0.23 |\n",
    "|United States | 0.15 |\n",
    "|China | 0.09 |\n",
    "|Ontario | 0.065 to 0.13 |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "From a purely cost-of-generation perspective, renewables  make sense almost everywhere in the world. Of course that is only part of the picture...as I'm sure you've heard, the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow. Expansion of renewable energies has become a **storage and distribution problem**. This problem was foreseen and has been the topic of discussion for an extended period. See, for example, the review articles: "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "[Benson & co-workers Energy & Environ. Sci. 2013, 6, 2804-2810](https://pubs-rsc-org.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/en/content/articlelanding/2013/ee/c3ee41973h)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/c3ee41973h-f1.gif\">"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "[Liu & co-workers Chem. Rev. 2011, 111, 3577-3613](https://pubs-acs-org.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/doi/full/10.1021/cr100290v)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/cr-2010-00290v_0017.jpeg\">"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "From the perspective of this course, this sets the **big picture**. This problem is what motivates what many of us do, but it does not at all indicate **what** we do. Our goals in this course are to understand the details of electrochemistry such that you are equipped to participate in this increasingly important area of technology."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 2. Electrochemistry \n",
    "So how does this all relate to electrochemistry? **Electrochemistry** has become a very hot topic in political, economic and social conversations because there is no single solution to the energy problem. There is a common thread that is woven through many of the most promising sustainable energy technologies: **electrons**. Wind power, solar power, and even nuclear power convert their respective sources of energy into *electrical energy*. \n",
    "\n",
    "There are plenty of technologies available to convert electrical energy into some alternative form. On the chemistry frontier we pursue chemical energy (ie, manipulating chemical bonds). This can be accomplished using hydrogen fuel cells, methanol fuel cells, ethanol fuel cells, formic acid fuel cells...lithium-ion batteries, Zn-ion batteries, lithium-air batteries, sodium-batteries...the list could go on. Which technology is the best? Which one will lead to the desired sustainable energy economy? \n",
    "\n",
    "In short, there is no single answer. Individual technologies have individual **energy** and **power** needs. The correct technology depends on these considerations. To understand the differences, we need to start considering chemistry."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "[Aurbach & coworkers Materials Today 2014, 17, 110-121.](https://www-sciencedirect-com.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/science/article/pii/S1369702114000741)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/1-s2.0-S1369702114000741-gr1_lrg.jpg\" width=40%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Thermodynamics \n",
    "Thermodynamics tells us how much energy any given technology can store. The above diagram considers this aspect using the approximate **energy** needs required to make selected technologies viable. Electrochemical technologies can be rated based on their **specific energy**.\n",
    "\n",
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\">\n",
    "<b>Specific Energy</b>: the amount of energy that can be stored by a given material per unit of mass (strictly, <i>specific</i> means per unit mass; the same quantity per unit volume is an energy <i>density</i>, and this course uses both)\n",
    "\n",
    "<b>Common units</b>: W $\\cdot$ h $\\cdot$ kg $^{-1}$ (specific energy), W$\\cdot$h$\\cdot$L$^{-1}$ (energy density). Note that mA$\\cdot$h$\\cdot$kg$^{-1}$ is charge per mass, which is a <b>specific capacity</b>, not a specific energy.\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-danger\">\n",
    "<b>Take the time to learn and understand units! </b>\n",
    "\n",
    "Units are your first line of defense against mistakes when working with numbers. If you write out and equation and your units don't match up, you've made a mistake. It cannot be stressed enough: always, always, ALWAYS write out your units!\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "    \n",
    "The units above may be somewhat familiar to everyone because we see units like mAh on our phones and laptops. However, what do these values really signify? These are units of energy, meaning that they have a relationship to all other units of energy. A fundamental relationship is defined as: operation of a circuit at one Watt (W) for 1 second requires one Joule (J) of energy:\n",
    "\n",
    "$ \\large 1 W \\cdot 1 s = 1 J $\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, a Watt-hour of energy (Wh) is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large 1 Wh  = 3.6 kJ\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "And 1 kilowatt hour is simply 1000 times this:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large 1 kWh  = 3.6 MJ\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "If for some reason you insist on using calories as units of energy, fundamental relationships allow you to perform similar conversion:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large 1 Wh  = 859.8 cal\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "If you fancy yourself a physical chemist, you may desire the use of electron volts (eV; the energy transferred to an electron when applying a potential difference of 1 V under vacuum):\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large 1 Ws = 1 J = 6.242×10^{18} eV\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "Remember...always inspect the UNITS you are using! This becomes particularly important when physical constants are incorporated into equations."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Kinetics\n",
    "Kinetics tells us the rate at which a reaction takes place. In electrochemistry this is captured by the magnitude of **electric currents** passed through a circuit. These electrons require application of some force to encourage their flow, which is supplied in the form of a **voltage**. In our context, **electrochemical potential** represents this voltage. The **power** is the product of the magnitude of current and the voltage, which can be represented as the **specific power**:\n",
    "\n",
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-success\">\n",
    "<b>Specific Power</b>: the rate of energy delivery by a given material per unit of mass (per unit volume it is a power <i>density</i>)\n",
    "\n",
    "<b>Common units</b>: W$\\cdot$kg$^{-1}$ (specific power), W$\\cdot$L$^{-1}$ (power density)\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "From an equation perspective, specific power (P) is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large P = \\frac{E \\cdot i}{m}\n",
    "$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Every technology, and every individual material has unique properties that influence both **specific power** and **specific energy**. More power lets your Tesla accelerate faster, while greater energy allows you to drive farther before recharging. There are two fundamental ways to accomplish this: \n",
    "1. add more battery cells and connect them in parallel/series to satisfy the requirements (at the expense of \\$\\$\\$ and mass) \n",
    "2. redesign the materials used in the battery to increase their **specific** energy and/or power.\n",
    "\n",
    "The specific energy and power of various technologies can be readily compared using **Ragone plots**. Inspect the generalized Ragone plot below and note that:\n",
    "1. the scales tend to be logarithmic\n",
    "2. the linear slopes represent the time to charge/discharge a given energy at a given power (ie, how long you can expect your phone to last, or your vehicle to drive)\n",
    "3. the goal is to move to the top-right corner, right where internal combustion engines are!\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/Ragone1.jpg\">"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Real-world behavior\n",
    "The example above is generalized: the ovals represent approximate behavior that can be expected from an individual technology. In reality, any given battery cell or fuel cell will present itself as a curve on the Ragone plot.\n",
    "\n",
    "As tends to be the case in chemistry, thermodynamics is fairly straightforward but kinetics quickly becomes complicated. Specific energy is dictated by the nature of the chemical reaction taking place. It *should* be constant. Specific power can be limited by reaction kinetics, mass transfer limitations, or external resistances. The situation can become complicated quickly, as we will repeatedly see in this course.\n",
    "\n",
    "Electrochemical cells obey **Ohm's law**:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large E = i \\cdot R\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "A given technology provides a resistance (R) to electron transfer. The current flow, and therefore the power, can be increased by increasing the voltage. In the context of a battery, this is represented by the **C-rate** and **capacity** (C) of the cell (both of which will be discussed in more detail in the future):\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large C-rate = \\frac{i}{C}\n",
    "$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For a limited region of performance, one is able to increase the current without having much impact on the overall capacity. At some point, however, increases in current begin to reduce capacity:\n",
    "\n",
    "[Goodenough & co-workers J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2015, 137, 2548-2554](https://pubs-acs-org.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/doi/10.1021/ja510347s)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/chargerates.png\" width=30%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "A more realistic form of the Ragone plot therefore looks like this:\n",
    "\n",
    "[McCloskey J. Phys. Chem. Lett., 2015, 6, 3592](https://pubs-acs-org.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/doi/pdf/10.1021/acs.jpclett.5b01813)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/ragone3.png\" width=30%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-info\">\n",
    "<b>Activity:</b> Pick a device you own that runs on a battery: a phone, a laptop, a cordless drill, an electric bike.\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "Estimate where it would sit on the Ragone plot above, and say which axis its designers were optimising. Then say what they gave up to get there.\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "- Device: ANSWER\n",
    "- Roughly where on the plot: ANSWER\n",
    "- Optimised for: ANSWER &nbsp;&nbsp; at the cost of: ANSWER"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "- Every device has a thermodynamic limit: it can only move so far up the **specific energy** axis\n",
    "- Every device can be operated at very low **power**, where it tends to achieve close to its theoretical energy\n",
    "- Sudden curvature in the line occurs near some **specific power**, signalling a fundamental limit in the rate (current) transferrable by that device \n",
    "\n",
    "The important concept here is **resistance** in the electrochemical cell. Flowing electrical current through a resistance results in thermal energy loss - less electrical energy can be extracted because the cell loses energy as heat (remember that first law!). Additional complications can also arise, such as harmful side reactions or decomposition of the active materials."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 3. Types of Electrochemical Devices\n",
    "\n",
    "This general introduction has hopefully convinced you that the only solution to our global energy problem is the development of a vast array of technologies, with each targeted at specific societal needs. The major technologies available are briefly summarized below. The terminology, technology, and experimental technique introduced below will all be covered in more detail in a subsequent lesson. They are presented here to help you visualize our path forward."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Capacitors, Supercapacitors, Ultracapacitors\n",
    "\n",
    "Devices that store energy using the **electrochemical double layer**. These devices rely upon **migration** of ions rather than chemical reactions. Such circumvention of **electron transfer kinetics** enables capacitors to operate at **very high power**, but **very low energy**. These devices are often characterized by a combination of **cyclic voltammetry**, **chronopotentiometry** and **chronoamperometry**.\n",
    "\n",
    "[Pope & co-workers ACS Nano 2017, 11, 10077-10087](https://pubs-acs-org.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/doi/abs/10.1021/acsnano.7b04467)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/capacitor.png\" width=40%>\n",
    "\n",
    "In **cyclic voltammetry** (panel **a** above), the relationship between **double layer capacitance** ($C_{dl}$) and **double layer charging current** ($i_{dl}$) is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large i_{dl} = \\nu \\cdot C_{dl}\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "In **chronopotentiometry** (panel **b** above), the current is held constant such that the electrochemical potential ($E$) changes as a function of time ($t$). For an ideal capacitor charged at a constant current ($i$), this gives a linear ramp:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large E = \\frac{i \\cdot t}{C_{dl}}\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "In **chronoamperometry**, the potential ($E$) is instead stepped to a new value and held constant, such that the double layer charging current ($i_{dl}$) decays as a function of time ($t$):\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large i_{dl} = \\frac{E}{R}e^{\\frac{-t}{R C_{dl}}}\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Ohm's law** is critically important here!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-info\">\n",
    "<b>Activity:</b> Three device classes were introduced above: capacitors, batteries and electrocatalysis cells.\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "For each one, name the single physical process that sets its energy, and the single process that sets its power. Then say which of the two you would attack first if you were asked to improve it.\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "| Device | Sets the energy | Sets the power | I would attack |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Capacitor | ANSWER | ANSWER | ANSWER |\n",
    "| Battery | ANSWER | ANSWER | ANSWER |\n",
    "| Electrocatalysis cell | ANSWER | ANSWER | ANSWER |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Strategies** to improve capacitors include:\n",
    "1. Increase the voltage window\n",
    "2. Increase $C_{dl}$\n",
    "3. Increase surface area\n",
    "\n",
    "Generally, this involves exploration of new **electrolytes**, new solvents, new solid state materials (**high $\\kappa$ dielectrics**), or new fabrication techniques.\n",
    "\n",
    "The behavior of these new materials are compared using Ragone plots:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/capacitors2.png\" width=30%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Batteries\n",
    "\n",
    "Storage of energy in chemical bonds by oxidizing/reducing a pair of (frequently) solid state materials. Batteries can be classified as **primary** (single use) or **secondary** (rechargable). They have a maximum **operating voltage** that establishes a **specific energy**. Their electron transfer characteristics are a complex mixture of **electron transfer kinetics**, **ionic diffusion**, and **chemical stability**. \n",
    "\n",
    "The basic concept of a battery is that a chemical reaction taking place at the **anode**, where the thermodynamics of the cell are represented by the **standard reduction potential** $E_a^0$, is paired to a chemical reaction at the cathode, where the thermodynamics are represented by the **standard reduction potential** $E_c^0$. The overall reaction is then represented by $E_{cell}^0$, which is the difference of the two reactions:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large E_{cell}^0 = E_c^0 - E_a^0\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "These standard reduction potentials are linked to the Gibbs energy of the reactions:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "\\large \\Delta G^0 = -n F E_{cell}^0\n",
    "$"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "From a practical perspective, a battery is structured like this:\n",
    "\n",
    "[Goodenough & Park J. Am. Chem. Soc. 2013, 135, 1167-1176](https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/ja3091438)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/battery.png\" width=40%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The active materials at the anode and cathode can be classified as **intercalation** materials (left side of figure below) or **conversion** materials (right side of figure below).\n",
    "\n",
    "[Materials Today 2015, 18, 252](https://www-sciencedirect-com.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/science/article/pii/S1369702114004118?via%3Dihub)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/batteries.jpg\" width=50%>\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/batterycharging.png\" width=50%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## (Photo)electrocatalysis Cells\n",
    "\n",
    "An alternative to capacitors and batteries is the synthesis of fuels. There is a vast array of possibilities, from electrocatalysis to photoelectrocatalysis, from hydrogen to ammonia. These function using an electrochemical cell similar to that in batteries, but the electrochemical reactions yield a bulk product that can be collected and stored.\n",
    "\n",
    "[Bisquert & co-workers Energy & Environ. Sci. 2015, 8, 2851](https://pubs-rsc-org.proxy.lib.uwaterloo.ca/en/content/articlehtml/2015/ee/c5ee01822f)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/pec.png\" width=50%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The selection of fuels is largely judged by energy density of the fuel and ease of storage. A comparison of common fuels is available through the US Energy Information Administration:\n",
    "\n",
    "[US Energy Information Administration](https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=9991)\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src=\"../Resources/images/density.png\" width=50%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# Final Assignment (that you should start now)\n",
    "\n",
    "A final assignment in this course involves reading the scientific literature and reporting on it. \n",
    "\n",
    "Start thinking about what interests **YOU** and start browsing the literature **TODAY**. The deadline may be far away, but doing the assignment gradually over the span of several months will provide you valuable opportunities to reflect as you learn (and will avoid significant stress as the end of semester approaches)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Recommended Resources:\n",
    "http://www.webofknowledge.com \n",
    "- a complete database of academic literature with powerful search algorithms. Can be tricky because you need to learn the key words that researchers use.\n",
    "\n",
    "http://scholar.google.com \n",
    "\n",
    "- very rapid and efficient searching of all academic literature (books, journals, presentations and abstracts). Aggressive algorithms make it a good starting point when you don't speak the research language, but it tends to yield too much information.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Review articles\n",
    "These types of articles are an excellent starting point for exploring fields of research. Here are some leads to get you started:\n",
    "\n",
    "##### Electrocatalysis\n",
    "- Alsac, E.P.; Bodappa, N.; Whittingham, A.; Liu, Y.; de Lazzari, A.; Smith, R. D. L. Structure–property correlations for analysis of heterogeneous electrocatalysts Chem. Phys. Rev. 2021, 2, 031306.\n",
    "- Chen, J. G.; Crooks, R. M.; Seefeldt, L. C.; Bren, K. L.; Morris Bullock, R.; Darensbourg, M. Y.; Holland, P. L.; Hoffman, B.; Janik, M. J.; Jones, A. K.; et al. Beyond Fossil Fuel-Driven Nitrogen Transformations. Science 2018, 360, 873.\n",
    "- Huang, X.; Groves, J. T. Oxygen Activation and Radical Transformations in Heme Proteins and Metalloporphyrins. Chem. Rev. 2018, 118, 2491–2553.\n",
    "- Francke, R.; Schille, B.; Roemelt, M. Homogeneously Catalyzed Electroreduction of Carbon Dioxide Methods, Mechanisms, and Catalysts. Chem. Rev. 2018, 118, 4631–4701.\n",
    "- Yan, D.; Li, Y.; Huo, J.; Chen, R.; Dai, L.; Wang, S.; Yan, D.; Li, Y.; Huo, J.; Chen, R.; et al. Defect Chemistry of Nonprecious-Metal Electrocatalysts for Oxygen Reactions. Adv. Mater. 2017, 29, 1606459.\n",
    "- Hwang, J.; Rao, R. R.; Giordano, L.; Katayama, Y.; Yu, Y.; Shao-Horn, Y. Perovskites in Catalysis and Electrocatalysis. Science 2017, 358, 751–756.\n",
    "- Zhu, C.; Fu, S.; Shi, Q.; Du, D.; Lin, Y. Single Atom Electrocatalysts. Angew. Chem. Int. Ed. 2017, 56, 13944–13960.\n",
    "- Weekes, D. M.; Salvatore, D. A.; Reyes, A.; Huang, A.; Berlinguette, C. P. Electrolytic CO2 Reduction in a Flow Cell. Acc. Chem. Res. 2018, 51, 910–918.\n",
    "\n",
